  <result aggregation-status="0" type="2">
   <title>
    <narrative xml:lang="EN">Output 7.3. Increased capacity of member States to set adequate wages and promote decent working time</narrative>
   </title>
   <description>
    <narrative xml:lang="EN">Following the adoption of the Omnibus Law on Employment Creation (Law No.11/2020), which reforms the entire labour code, the Government published a new Wage Regulation (Government Regulation No. 36) in February 2021.  

The new Wage Regulation includes various changes to the minimum wage system, including a reduction in the number of minimum wage rates and the use of new evidence-based methodologies to assess the needs of the workers and their families and economic factors.  

Another Government regulation (Regulation No.35) establishes that the lowest hourly wage for part-time workers is set higher than the monthly wage for full-time workers. This aims to protect workers/labourers against job insecurity. Indeed, there are no regulations on part-time workers in Indonesia, so clear regulations are needed, especially on wages, to protect part-time workers and include working time arrangements beyond full-time. Government Regulation No.36/2021 is based on the many consultations with the ILO, and a significant amount of technical inputs to the government was provided. The ILO conducted a workshop on the  impact of minimum wages and wage policy monitoring in 2020. With support from the Government of the Netherlands, the ILO also provided technical assistance on the use of evidence to set minimum wages by addressing the needs of the workers and their families alongside economic factors. Some of these concepts have been included in Government Regulation No.36/2021.
A consultation between the government and the ILO was held on November 24, 2020, where the Government briefed the ILO on the the Omnibus Law on Employment, and where ILO provided advice on technical matters related to minimum wages. However, not all technical inputs were incorporated in the Regulations. In particular, the ILO emphasized the importance of full consultation with social partners and social dialogue to set adequate minimum wages.
There are other areas for improvement, primarily to ensure effective implementation of the minimum wages during the pandemic. ILO is working both at the national and provincial levels to establish evidence-based policies related to wages.</narrative>
   </description>
